WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:02.901
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated writer

00:00:02.901 --> 00:00:05.471
and comedian who is the
longest-serving "Weekend Update"

00:00:05.471 --> 00:00:07.273
anchor in the history
of "Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:10.075
which returns this week
with host Adam Driver

00:00:10.075 --> 00:00:12.212
and musical guest
Olivia Rodrigo.

00:00:12.212 --> 00:00:14.447
His new scripted podcast,
"Trust Fall,"

00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:16.215
a photo he was not
in the room...

00:00:16.215 --> 00:00:19.752
[ Laughter ]
Like, how would that happen?

00:00:19.752 --> 00:00:23.723
[ Laughter ]
Just be honest.

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:26.492 line:0%
[ Laughter ]
Just be honest.

00:00:26.492 --> 00:00:28.094 line:0%
"Trust Fall" is available
on Audible.

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:31.130 line:0%
Please welcome back to the show
our very good friend Colin Jost!

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.666
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:36.669
[ Up-tempo music plays ]

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:48.981
♪♪

00:00:48.981 --> 00:00:51.517
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:53.786
-Thank you very much.
Thank you.

00:00:53.786 --> 00:00:57.556
-You know, they're applauding me
for having the courage

00:00:57.556 --> 00:00:59.292
to call you out.
-Yeah.

00:00:59.292 --> 00:01:01.995
What -- What looks fake?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:03.897
-Yeah.

00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:06.065
-Have you ever seen
me and Wanda together,

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:07.867
and we didn't look like this?
-Yeah, that's true.

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.503
You're almost always leaning.
She's taking a good selfie.

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:12.805
I say, now that I see you in
person, you do look like that.

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:15.474
-See?
-Um, uh, It's great.

00:01:15.474 --> 00:01:18.244
-It's not far off.
-I see you, uh -- It's my --

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:20.380
-You work -- We work together.
-We work together.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.548
-We were in the same building.
How do you get to work?

00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:25.618
-How do I get to work?
The, uh, show, uh, sends a car.

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:27.953
I take a car to work.
How do you get to work?

00:01:27.953 --> 00:01:30.322
-Uh, I bike.
I guess I'm just maybe

00:01:30.322 --> 00:01:31.924
a little more man of the people.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.924 --> 00:01:33.960
-You bike.
-I bike, I bike.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.130
-What do you do, a Citi Bike?
-No, I buy bikes.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.266
And then I just throw them away
when I get here.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:42.001
[ Laughter ]
So I just, you know...

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:44.270
-Yes, man of the people.
-Yeah.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:45.404
-You just, like,
leave them on the street?

00:01:45.404 --> 00:01:48.007
Because there's not, like,
a bike trash can.

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:50.309
-Yeah, well, I mean --
I, well, you know,

00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:53.146
it's a Citi Bike-like system.
-But it's just not Citi Bike.

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:54.848
-But I just -- yeah,
but I just throw them away.

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:56.883
And I don't, you know --
I mean, it's --

00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:59.852
I don't leave them in a shape
where they could be reused.

00:01:59.852 --> 00:02:01.487
[ Laughter ]

00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:03.889
You know that big giant pile
of broken bikes

00:02:03.889 --> 00:02:05.457
that's right outside?
-That's your bike?

00:02:05.457 --> 00:02:08.127
-That's my bikes.
Haven't you ever wondered?

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:09.862
You thought it was one of those
cool sculptures they put up

00:02:09.862 --> 00:02:12.365
sometimes at 30 Rock.
-I did. I thought it was Koons.

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:15.201
-Oh, I get Koons a lot.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.903
-That's great. That's your
favorite kind of bike?

00:02:17.903 --> 00:02:19.505
The one you just do once?
-Yeah.

00:02:19.505 --> 00:02:20.906
The disposable bikes.
-Yeah.

00:02:20.906 --> 00:02:23.142
-I think I'm
an environmental guy, you know.

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:25.244
You guys know that about me.
But I bike my bike.

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:26.412
And I don't know
if you've noticed now --

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:28.181
I feel like,
in the last year --

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:29.649
maybe you guys
have noticed this, too --

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:32.084
that somehow
they've allowed pedicabs

00:02:32.084 --> 00:02:35.755
to just blast the loudest music
you've ever heard.

00:02:35.755 --> 00:02:38.190
[ Laughter ]
And for some reason,

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:41.660
I find that they're all
exclusively playing "Despacito."

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.896
-Yeah.
-Like, it's like

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:46.498
it's a very festive version
of hell, you know?

00:02:46.498 --> 00:02:49.602
Like, that's all you hear.
And it's so loud,

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:50.903
and I will just be biking,
I'm like,

00:02:50.903 --> 00:02:52.505
"Oh, what's approaching me
from behind?

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:54.540
Is it the entire
Puerto Rican Day parade?"

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.109
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:58.410
-No, it's a single
empty pedicab.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:02:59.646
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:01.915
-And I realized that all
the speakers project out.

00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:03.215
So if you're in
the actual pedicab,

00:03:03.215 --> 00:03:05.184
it's relatively quiet.
-Yeah.

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:07.019
You're, like, you think
you're on your way

00:03:07.019 --> 00:03:09.255
to a club 20 blocks away
that's playing "Despacito."

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:11.157
-Exactly. I mean, the dream.
-The dream.

00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:12.858
-We all, you know...
-You're like, "Faster!"

00:03:12.858 --> 00:03:14.827
-Yeah.
-Do you ever, in those moments,

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:17.430
like, when you see
an empty pedicab blasting music,

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.064
do you try to step in and go,
like, "I might --"

00:03:19.064 --> 00:03:21.266
again, this isn't
my business per se,

00:03:21.266 --> 00:03:24.170
but I might have a note,
"I feel like, by blasting this,

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.906
people don't want to be
in your pedicab."

00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:30.076
-Maybe -- I think it's aimed
at, like, a full family,

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:32.311
that the dad is so upset
at everyone

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:33.879
and he's like,
"Just get in the --

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:35.215
I don't want to hear
any of you anymore.

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:38.051
I just need 'Despacito'
right now."

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:39.351
[ Laughter ]
I think that's

00:03:39.351 --> 00:03:42.221
what "Despacito" means --
"Desperate Dad."

00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:46.025
-They say, "'Despacito' times
call for 'Despacito' measures."

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:47.560
-That's right, that's right.
You know.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:50.462
-We know this famous saying.
-Famously.

00:03:50.462 --> 00:03:52.131
-You're back in the swing
at the show.

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:53.399
It's been lovely
to have you guys back.

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:55.033
We missed a few
because of the writers' strike.

00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:56.836
And now it's been wonderful.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:56.836 --> 00:03:58.704
-It's great.
It's been very nice.

00:03:58.704 --> 00:04:00.707
-Love the new cast.
Love the young cast.

00:04:00.707 --> 00:04:02.975
-I love the new cast.
They're great.

00:04:02.975 --> 00:04:05.278
The last couple years
have been really great.

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:07.347
They're all fun.
I love hanging out with them.

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:09.582
They're doing great on the show.

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:12.151
And it's very nice to be back.

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:14.720
We got Adam Driver
who's, like, fantastic.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.125
Really, really very good
at the show.

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:21.894
-I'm happy about the outcome
for the real person,

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:25.864
but Bowen as George Santos
was a real gift to the show.

00:04:25.864 --> 00:04:28.134
-That was so --
and to all of America, really.

00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:30.336
-All of America.
And you know,

00:04:30.336 --> 00:04:32.671
maybe not the most obvious
casting in the beginning,

00:04:32.671 --> 00:04:34.106
but you guys worked
on this together.

00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:35.874
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:35.874 --> 00:04:38.244
And he -- You know,
Bowen is fantastic.

00:04:38.244 --> 00:04:41.247
And I feel like I don't think
it's necessarily goodbye

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:43.215
to George Santos.
I feel like it's, "Hello,

00:04:43.215 --> 00:04:46.052
a thousand documentaries
about him," is my guess.

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:47.186
I feel like
we'll probably see him.

00:04:47.186 --> 00:04:48.821
Like, there's almost no chance
he's not

00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:51.591
in a Super Bowl commercial
for, like, pistachios.

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:54.827
-Yeah,
and someone's like, "George!"

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:57.129
That definitely is a line.
-And he's like, "What?"

00:04:57.129 --> 00:04:58.764
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.764 --> 00:05:02.268
-Pistachios.
-I'm Mr. Pistachio.

00:05:02.268 --> 00:05:03.469
-[ Chuckles ]

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:05.904
And then he also, like --
I do feel like

00:05:05.904 --> 00:05:07.973
what I thought about
George Santos, too,

00:05:07.973 --> 00:05:11.411
is that almost, like, we should
give him more credit

00:05:11.411 --> 00:05:14.146
because he was able
to get elected,

00:05:14.146 --> 00:05:16.549
but he only -- he used all
the campaign stuff on,

00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:19.385
like, Botox and, you know,
like Hermès.

00:05:19.385 --> 00:05:22.488
Like, these other people
spent it all on the campaign

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:24.256
and couldn't win.
-Yeah.

00:05:24.256 --> 00:05:26.225
-Isn't that kind of impressive?
-Well, of course you won.

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:28.027
You con those people
out of their money,

00:05:28.027 --> 00:05:29.328
and you used it
to run against us.

00:05:29.328 --> 00:05:31.163
He's like, "No."
-"No."

00:05:31.163 --> 00:05:33.766
Would you have rather --
wouldn't you rather him

00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:36.869
look like real, like,
you know, sauced up

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:39.505
instead of having to see, like,
another campaign ad from him?

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:42.341
It's cool, you know?
-It is very -- Again,

00:05:42.341 --> 00:05:44.743
the best thing about him is
the lies didn't seem impressive.

00:05:44.743 --> 00:05:47.046
Like, he lied about things
that you wouldn't think

00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:49.148
another person would vote
for you if you were like,

00:05:49.148 --> 00:05:50.582
"Oh, he played
college volleyball.

00:05:50.582 --> 00:05:52.218
He's my guy."
-Oh, yeah.

00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:54.821
Yeah, I never thought
I would try --

00:05:54.821 --> 00:05:56.588
that someone would try
to impress me

00:05:56.588 --> 00:05:59.391
with college volleyball.
-And Santos did, and it worked.

00:05:59.391 --> 00:06:00.793
-And that's
why he's a visionary.

00:06:00.793 --> 00:06:02.528
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:05.064
-He also does make --
I feel like he made people

00:06:05.064 --> 00:06:06.832
feel better
about themselves, too.

00:06:06.832 --> 00:06:08.767
Like, you know,
when you saw that going on,

00:06:08.767 --> 00:06:10.937
you're like, "Oh,
I'm not that bad of a person."

00:06:10.937 --> 00:06:12.372
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.372 --> 00:06:14.374
-Adam Driver
was in "Marriage Story"

00:06:14.374 --> 00:06:15.941
with your wife,
Scarlett Johansson.

00:06:15.941 --> 00:06:17.643
-That's right.
-Is that a weird thing?

00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:19.111
[ Cheers and applause ]
Give it up for "Marriage Story."

00:06:19.111 --> 00:06:20.746
-Yeah, great movie.
-Because that was, like,

00:06:20.746 --> 00:06:22.115
maybe one of the best movies
ever made

00:06:22.115 --> 00:06:24.016
about the dissolution
of a marriage.

00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:27.152
-Yes, I guess it is --
It's odd to meet someone

00:06:27.152 --> 00:06:29.655
who played a person
who divorced your wife.

00:06:29.655 --> 00:06:31.958
That's a weird -- 'Cause I guess
when you date an actor,

00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:33.593
I've heard, you know,
they sometimes

00:06:33.593 --> 00:06:35.561
have to kiss people in movies
or whatever.

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:37.763
-You found out, though,
after you said "I do," right?

00:06:37.763 --> 00:06:39.865
-The minute after.
-Yeah.

00:06:39.865 --> 00:06:41.667
She's like,
"Now that it's official,

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:43.703
I have some things
to tell you about acting."

00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:46.238
-She's like,
"Look at this list!"

00:06:46.238 --> 00:06:48.774
I'm kidding.
I don't keep a list.

00:06:48.774 --> 00:06:50.542
Um...
[ Laughter ]

00:06:50.542 --> 00:06:55.581
Uh, yeah, it is weird to meet
someone that that was played --

00:06:55.581 --> 00:06:57.283
because you're like, "Oh, I was
in a movie with your wife."

00:06:57.283 --> 00:06:59.551
"Oh, what did you do?"
"I divorced her."

00:06:59.551 --> 00:07:02.188
And by the way, I should have
said "spoiler alert,"

00:07:02.188 --> 00:07:04.423
but "Marriage Story" is
actually about divorce.

00:07:04.423 --> 00:07:05.858
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:05.858 --> 00:07:07.927
-So if you thought like, "Oh,
it's a 'Father of the Bride'..."

00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:10.062
-You kind of know right away.
-Yeah, right.

00:07:10.062 --> 00:07:11.763
They get that out really fast.
-Yeah.

00:07:11.763 --> 00:07:13.365
Like, pretty much, like --
-The first line is,

00:07:13.365 --> 00:07:15.867
"What are you doing?!
I hate you!"

00:07:15.867 --> 00:07:19.538
-Um, we saw each other
at, uh,

00:07:19.538 --> 00:07:21.540
the Natural History Museum gala.
-Oh, yes.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:24.009
Seth emceed the gala
and was fantastic.

00:07:24.009 --> 00:07:26.879
-Thank you.
-Great job. Excellent work.

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:30.049
-You sort of went --
You sort of went viral

00:07:30.049 --> 00:07:32.518
for a red-carpet moment.
-Did I? Oh, wow.

00:07:32.518 --> 00:07:34.453
-I want to show it first.
And then I want you

00:07:34.453 --> 00:07:36.355
to explain what happened.
-Okay, okay.

00:07:36.355 --> 00:07:37.857
Here's a...
-Okay, okay.

00:07:37.857 --> 00:07:41.460
[ Indistinct conversations ]

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.530
[ Camera shutters clicking ]

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.767
[ Indistinct conversations
continue ]

00:07:47.767 --> 00:07:50.469
[ Laughter ]

00:07:50.469 --> 00:07:54.106
-So, what happened?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:56.475 --> 00:07:57.743
-So, I don't understand.

00:07:57.743 --> 00:08:00.212
Are you implying
that someone gave me

00:08:00.212 --> 00:08:03.882
some sort of gummy mushroom
right before

00:08:03.882 --> 00:08:06.685
I went to the red carpet?
Is that what you're implying?

00:08:06.685 --> 00:08:10.088
-And that you froze like a deer
in oncoming traffic,

00:08:10.088 --> 00:08:12.858
to the degree that your wife
had to lean over

00:08:12.858 --> 00:08:16.162
in front of a camera crew
and be like, "Are you okay?"

00:08:16.162 --> 00:08:18.297
[ Laughter ]

00:08:18.297 --> 00:08:21.200
-Are you implying that
when a thousand flashbulbs

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.768
went off in front of my face,

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:26.171
that I thought I was staring
into the dawn of the cosmos...

00:08:26.171 --> 00:08:28.774
[ Laughter ]
...and that I saw,

00:08:28.774 --> 00:08:32.678
fully realized, my entire past
and future in one moment,

00:08:32.678 --> 00:08:36.882
until my wife had to rip me
out of the space-time continuum

00:08:36.882 --> 00:08:40.285
and say, "Are you okay?"
[ Laughter ]

00:08:40.285 --> 00:08:42.488
I don't know
what you're implying, but...

00:08:42.488 --> 00:08:44.056
-Can I tell you something?
-Yeah.

00:08:44.056 --> 00:08:45.858
-But I've seen that face before.
-Right.

00:08:45.858 --> 00:08:48.094
-Because we work together,
and I want to show it

00:08:48.094 --> 00:08:49.462
one more time
and let everybody here know

00:08:49.462 --> 00:08:51.697
this is the face Colin
would make if I'd be like,

00:08:51.697 --> 00:08:54.233
"Hey, did you finish
writing the cold open?"

00:08:54.233 --> 00:08:56.502
[ Laughter ]

00:08:56.502 --> 00:08:58.004
-[ Chuckles ]

00:08:58.004 --> 00:09:01.173
[ Laughter ]

00:09:06.579 --> 00:09:08.581
[ Applause ]

00:09:08.581 --> 00:09:11.017
-We'll be right back
with more Colin after this!

